    <item>
      <title>Vulkan 1.4.368 Released With New Extensions From Valve &amp; NVIDIA</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Vulkan-1.4.368</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Vulkan-1.4.368</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 07 Nov 2026 10:53:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VULKAN" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/vulkan.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Vulkan 1.4.368 is out as the newest weekly spec update for this high performance graphics and compute API.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;There are just six issues resolved in this spec update for internally raised issues. Overall a small update with just one week passing since the prior update. But there are two new extensions: VK_EXT_vertex_address_binding_unrestricted and VK_NV_internal_handle.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=vulkan_14368" alt="Vulkan 1.4.368 new extensions" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;VK_EXT_vertex_address_binding_unrestricted was drafted by Mike Blumenkrantz of Valve's Linux graphics team and prominent Mesa developer. This extension deals with vertex attribute updates:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"When optimizing vertex attribute updates for a draw command which reads only from the updated offset, attribute data can be split off into a separate buffer for that draw. For this scenario, the vertex buffer data can be bound using `vkCmdBindVertexBuffers3KHR` such that only the effective offset read during vertex processing is non-negative in order to reduce memory because the calculated draw-time parameters will access valid data. Using this extension disables address validation for this scenario."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The other new extension this week is the VK_NV_internal_handle vendor extension. VK_NV_internal_handle allows applications and game engines to obtain opaque internal driver handles for certain Vulkan objects. These internal NVIDIA driver handles can then be passed to external APIs that accept them for different NVIDIA-focused use-cases.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;All the Vulkan 1.4.368 details can be found via &lt;a href="https://github.com/KhronosGroup/Vulkan-Docs/commit/fa30d4e312e843193601be7cb926c7e25d9ec885"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Microsoft's Azure Linux 3.0.2022 Ships With More Than 470 Security Fixes</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Azure-Linux-3.0.2022</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Azure-Linux-3.0.2022</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 07 Nov 2026 10:37:42 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="MICROSOFT" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/microsoft.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Microsoft released Azure Linux 3.0.2022 on Friday night as the newest version of their in-house Linux distribution. Given the rampant AI/LLM-fueled bug reporting and security research, Azure Linux 3.0.2022 ends up incorporating more than 470 security fixes for different CVEs across the many open-source projects making up Azure Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The security fixes in this month's Azure Linux update range fro hardening of certificates due to the eMudhra incident to security fixes for the Linux kernel and other common open-source projects/ Plus some of Microsoft's own Azure components have also seen security fixes. More than 470 CVEs alone are mentioned in the 3.0.2022 change-log. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Azure Linux update also ships new Dockerfiles, an OpenVMM patch for confidential support, and fixing a handful of bugs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the new Azure Linux release via &lt;a href="https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/releases/tag/3.0.20261105-3.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Asahi Linux Makes Progress On Gaming, More Apple Silicon Upstreaming</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Asahi-Linux-7.3-Report</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Asahi-Linux-7.3-Report</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 07 Nov 2026 10:21:03 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="APPLE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/apple.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Given the recent Linux 7.3 upstream kernel release, the Asahi Linux project published a new status report to outline recent code they have upstreamed to the mainline kernel as well as other efforts taking shape in their downstream codebase. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Now midway into Q4'2026, the Asahi Linux project outlined some recent accomplishments and other efforts underway in getting modern Apple Silicon hardware working on Linux: &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- Asahi Linux developers have continued upstreaming more code to the Linux kernel, as we continue to regularly cover on Phoronix. There has been recent upstreaming work around audio as well as the initial Device Trees for the Apple M4 Pro and Max and M5 appearing on the Linux kernel mailing list. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- Apple M3 support is considered under general availability (GA) status now with the downstream Asahi Linux code. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The notable downside to the current Apple M3 support is the lack of GPU acceleration. Those reverse-engineering and working on the driver support for the Apple M3 have made some progress and have rendered a triangle but still there is a long road ahead. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- Thunderbolt support for Apple Silicon with Linux is now out for review on the Linux kernel mailing list. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- Gaming on Linux with Asahi is becoming more of a reality (well, on Apple M1/M2) when paired with the likes of EX and Wine. There's also progress on running games outside of their muvm for dealing with kernel page size differences: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=asahi_gaming_1" alt="Asahi gaming" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=asahi_gaming_2" alt="Asahi gaming Fallout" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The performance though of running the more recent games on Asahi Linux is described as "patchy". &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the recent Asaho Linux progress via the &lt;a href="https://asahilinux.org/2026/11/progress-report-7-3/"&gt;AsahiLinux.org blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>FreeBSD 15.2 Beta 1 Allows Experimental Cross-Building Of Base Packages On Linux/macOS</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/FreeBSD-15.2-Beta-1</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/FreeBSD-15.2-Beta-1</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 07 Nov 2026 00:04:20 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="BSD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/bsd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; FreeBSD 15.2 Beta 1 is out today as the release dance kicks off in working toward the next installment of FreeBSD 15. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;FreeBSD 15.2 brings dozens of security fixes raised in recent months from use-after-free and heap overflows to missing validations and remote code execution vectors. Unfortunately, quite a lot of security fixes in the era of AI/LLMs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;FreeBSD 15.2 introduces experimental support for cross-building base system packages on Linux and macOS hosts. This depends upon the pkg utility being manually setup on the build host. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;FreeBSD 15.2 on laptops adds a new "backlight_enable" rc.conf option to allow saving and restoring the backlight level across reboots. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With FreeBSD 15.2, Clang now defaults to using the LLVM linker ld.lld) unless explicitly overriden. LLVM 21.1.8 is pulled in for FreeBSD 15.2. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There are also many other packages updates like OpenSSH 10.4p1, OpenZFS 2.4.4, XZ 5.8.4, and more. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those using FreeBSD in the cloud with Amazon AWS, the EC2 desktop AMIs of FreeBSD now boot to a KDE desktop with XRDP enabled. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Last but not least are a variety of hardware driver fixes and new hardware ID additions. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the FreeBSD 15.2 changes at large can be found via the &lt;a href="https://www.freebsd.org/releases/15.2R/relnotes/"&gt;release notes&lt;/a&gt;. Details on today's beta one release can be found via the &lt;a href="https://lists.freebsd.org/archives/freebsd-stable/2026-November/004356.html"&gt;FreeBSD-stable mailing list&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;FreeBSD 15.2 is working toward an official release on 8 December while until then will be weekly betas followed by at least one release candidate over the next month.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ReactOS Lands Initial USB3 Driver Support</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/ReactOS-Initial-USB-3.0</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/ReactOS-Initial-USB-3.0</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 21:49:46 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; After years of work, ReactOS as the "open-source Windows" operating system striving for Windows program and driver compatibility now has an initial USB 3.0 driver stack in place. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Following last month's excitement of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/ReactOS-NTFS-Driver"&gt;ReactOS now install-able and used with its NTFS driver&lt;/a&gt;, the next major milestone for this open-source project is having a working USB3 stack. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=reactos_usb3" alt="ReactOS USB 3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/reactos/reactos/pull/9646"&gt;This merge&lt;/a&gt; landed the initial import of USB3 driver code and already with an eye toward eventually having USB4 in ReactOS: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"This has been a very long time coming, the origins of this driver traces back a few years but I've gone through many many iterations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;First off, Because we're doing a hackfest! I'm doing this in one giga sized PR. I've been daily driving this USB stack from Windows 11 for awhile and I've given versions of the binaries to a few people! It's quite stable. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There's a second PR after this one and DeviceProperty support is merged involving the USB4 Host router and USB4 Device router. Of course they depend on this though.."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Awesome job by the ReactOS crew continuing to advance this platform.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>A RISC-V Based TEE With AMD Zen 6 CPUs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Zen-6-TEE-RISC-V-Based</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Zen-6-TEE-RISC-V-Based</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 19:30:49 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; An interesting technical detail I noticed today from the latest AMD Linux activity is that Zen 6 processors apparently are featuring a RISC-V based Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) where in the past it was an ARM-based core. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A little nugget dropped out today in a Git commit with either a new Zen 6 feature or something that wasn't widely reported on for recent Zen 5 processors, but there's now a RISC-V-based TEE with Zen 6 CPUs at least. I haven't been able to find any other references to a RISC-V TEE/core implementation with AMD EPYC/Ryzen processors, but a &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/firmware/linux-firmware.git/commit/?id=c1c76b61ec873aa3145314b6c0273be93ab45596"&gt;commit&lt;/a&gt; today to linux-firmware.git makes mention of a RISC-V based TEE for Family 1Ah M80h as the upcoming AMD Ryzen Zen 6 client processors: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"amd_pmf: Add AMD PMF TA Firmware v4 for 1AH_M80H &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This adds the PMF TA FW v4 for the 1AH_M80H platform. The TA now runs on the RISC-V based TEE, uses a larger shared memory layout and supports an extended set of policy conditions, including up to 20 custom BIOS inputs."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A RISC-V based TEE. Traditionally the AMD Secure Processor / Platform Security Processor (PSP) was based on the Arm Cortex-A5 core with Arm TrustZone technology. Now seemingly new for next-gen Ryzen processors or otherwise just never mentioned for existing SoCs/APUs is a RISC-V based Trusted Execution Environment. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=amd_riscv_tee" alt="AMD RISC-V TEE" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In regards to today's firmware drop, it's for the AMD Platform Management Framework (PMF) Trusted Application (TA) for managing power, thermal, and system behavior. Great seeing the AMD Ryzen Zen 6 work getting squared away ahead of the products officially launching.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.4-rc2 Change Yields As Much As 162x Improvement For FAT32</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-rc2-FAT32</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-rc2-FAT32</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 16:21:38 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In addition to &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Faster-FAT32"&gt;a FAT32 optimization yielding as much as a 265x improvement&lt;/a&gt; that was merged for the Linux 7.4 cycle and separately &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Faster-FAT32-Linux-In-2027"&gt;another big FAT32 improvement expected for Linux 7.5&lt;/a&gt;, merged this week as part of the non-MM fixes for Linux 7.4-rc2 was another big FAT32 performance optimization. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Open-source developer Matheus Alves de Almeida continues working on enhancing the FAT32 performance with the Linux FAT driver. The work merged this week is for pre-allocating space before expanding within the fat_cont_expand() function. He explained in the patch: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The fat_cont_expand() implementation leaves allocation of new clusters to generic_cont_expand_simple(). This path does many small cluster allocations, harming performance. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Preallocate cluster space in fat_cont_expand(). Move preallocation to a new fat_preallocate() helper used by both fat_cont_expand() and fat_fallocate(FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE). Use a FAT-specific fat_cont_expand_simple() instead of generic_cont_expand_simple() to avoid rechecking the new size after clusters have been allocated."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261024013021.251898-1-matheus.aalmeida@inf.ufrgs.br/"&gt;The patch&lt;/a&gt; can deal some mighty nice improvements: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=linux_74rc2_fat32" alt="FAT32 benchmark of code merged for Linux 7.4-rc2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The biggest impact from this optimization work is for FAT32 file-systems with small cluster sizes on synchronous mounts. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Most are OCFS2 fixes. These address extended-attribute corruption, missing attributes and kernel BUGs, along with crashes during cluster configuration, lock migration, recovery and unmount. Extent movement is also fixed to validate its range against a stable file size. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Three FAT changes prevent a failed-write/FIBMAP race from spuriously making the filesystem read-only, correct directory link counts after rename, and speed up file expansion. The reported QEMU benchmarks show particularly large improvements on synchronous mounts. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A taint-string fix prevents an out-of-bounds read which can expose slab contents in the kernel log. There are also build fixes for OpenRISC modules on CPUs without hardware multiply and for Clang 24."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The work landed via the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261104202749.a5b9ab39d3169f80b5089a14@linux-foundation.org/"&gt;non-MM hotfixes pull request&lt;/a&gt; ahead of Linux 7.4-rc2 on Sunday.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Arch Linux vs. CachyOS vs. Omarchy 4.5 Performance Benchmarks</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 14:55:52 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_2_med" alt="Omarchy 4.5 desktop" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/CachyOS"&gt;CachyOS&lt;/a&gt; recently having rolled out its Linux 7.3 based kernel and various other improvements plus with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Omarchy-4.5-Released"&gt;Omarchy 4.5&lt;/a&gt; having been released this week, this article provides a look at how CachyOS and Omarchy are competing on the performance front relative to upstream Arch Linux.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_1_med" alt="Omarchy 4.5 Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Using the same &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Framework+Desktop"&gt;Framework Desktop&lt;/a&gt; computer with the AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395 "Strix Halo" with 64GB of RAM, integrated Radeon 8060S Graphics, and 2TB WD_BLACK SN7100 NVMe SSD, all three Linux distributions were tested on this system.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="https://phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/result.svgz" alt="Arch Linux vs. Cachy OS vs. Omarchy" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Arch Linux was installed using a generic archinstall setup with the KDE Plasma desktop, CachyOS was installed at its defaults and updated, and Omarchy 4.5 was also done with its stock install.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_3_med" alt="CachyOS" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS is on Linux 7.3 while Omarchy and Arch Linux upstream as of testing were still on Linux 7.2. All three are on GCC 16.2. Omarchy notably defaults to using the performance profile out-of-the-box while Arch Linux and CachyOS defaulted to balanced performance on this laptop. CachyOS notably also includes a lot of out-of-the-box optimizations from Zen 4+ optimized packages and other tuning.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This is our first look at how Omarchy is performing relative to Arch Linux and CachyOS, so let's have a look.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_4_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_4_med" alt="Arch Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/gravitymark-3840-x-2160-opengl.svgz" alt="GravityMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Renderer: OpenGL. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/gravitymark-3840-x-2160-vulkan.svgz" alt="GravityMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Renderer: Vulkan. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/gravitymark-3840-x-2160-vulkan-ray-tracing.svgz" alt="GravityMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Renderer: Vulkan Ray-Tracing. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For the demanding GravityMark OpenGL and Vulkan graphics benchmarks, CachyOS and Arch Linux were performing similarly with Mesa 26.2 on the Radeon 8060S Graphics of Strix Halo. Omarchy was the outlier in being slower than the other two with its Hyprland-based environment.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/xonotic-6144-x-2560-low.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 6144 x 2560, Effects Quality: Low. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/xonotic-6144-x-2560-high.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 6144 x 2560, Effects Quality: High. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/xonotic-6144-x-2560-ultra.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 6144 x 2560, Effects Quality: Ultra. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/xonotic-6144-x-2560-ultimate.svgz" alt="Xonotic benchmark with settings of Resolution: 6144 x 2560, Effects Quality: Ultimate. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Xonotic first person shooter was also the slowest on Omarchy 4.5. This was similar in other graphics tests too, presumably due to some overhead or lack of optimizations compared to KDE Plasma Wayland on Arch Linux and CachyOS.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/zstd-compression-19-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Compression Speed. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/zstd-compression-19-ds.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Decompression Speed. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/zstd-compression-19-long-mode-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Long Mode, Compression Speed. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/zstd-compression-19-long-mode-ds.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Long Mode, Decompression Speed. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Zstd as supplied by each operating system saw a slight lead for Omarchy in most of the Zstandard compression tests carried out, perhaps due to it using the performance profile by default rather than balanced performance on the other two.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/openapv-fast-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Fast, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/openapv-slow-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Slow, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/openapv-medium-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Medium, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/openapv-fastest-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Fastest, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Similarly, Omarchy 4.5 was fastest out-of-the-box for OpenAPV video encoding.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-3-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 3, Input: Bosphorus 4K. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-5-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 5, Input: Bosphorus 4K. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-8-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 8, Input: Bosphorus 4K. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-13-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 13, Input: Bosphorus 4K. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-3-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 3, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-5-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 5, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/svt-av1-preset-13-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 13, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS did pick up a win for SVT-AV1 encoding while Omarchy at its defaults also picked up some additional wins.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/x265-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="x265 benchmark with settings of Video Input: Bosphorus 4K. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/x265-bosphorus-1080p.svgz" alt="x265 benchmark with settings of Video Input: Bosphorus 1080p. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS was the fastest with the x265 video encoding performance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/7-zip-compression-compression-rating.svgz" alt="7-Zip Compression benchmark with settings of Test: Compression Rating. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/7-zip-compression-dr.svgz" alt="7-Zip Compression benchmark with settings of Test: Decompression Rating. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With 7-Zip supplied by each OS, CachyOS was delivering the fastest compression performance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/timed-apache-compilation-time-to-compile.svgz" alt="Timed Apache Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/timed-godot-game-engine-compilation-time-to-compile.svgz" alt="Timed Godot Game Engine Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/timed-llvm-compilation-ninja.svgz" alt="Timed LLVM Compilation benchmark with settings of Build System: Ninja. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-defconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: defconfig. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS was the fastest when it came to build times across a variety of open-source codebases.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/numpy-benchmark.svgz" alt="Numpy Benchmark benchmark with settings of . CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS and Omarchy were slightly faster than upstream Arch Linux for Numpy.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pytorch-cpu-16-resnet-50.svgz" alt="PyTorch benchmark with settings of Device: CPU, Batch Size: 16, Model: ResNet-50. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pytorch-cpu-32-resnet-50.svgz" alt="PyTorch benchmark with settings of Device: CPU, Batch Size: 32, Model: ResNet-50. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pytorch-cpu-64-resnet-50.svgz" alt="PyTorch benchmark with settings of Device: CPU, Batch Size: 64, Model: ResNet-50. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;PyTorch was the fastest on CachyOS albeit by very slim margins.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/clickhouse-1rhdlsr.svgz" alt="ClickHouse benchmark with settings of 100M Rows Hits Dataset, Latency, Second Run. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/clickhouse-1rhdltr.svgz" alt="ClickHouse benchmark with settings of 100M Rows Hits Dataset, Latency, Third Run. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Omarchy had a small lead in the ClickHouse database server.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-500-read-only.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 500, Mode: Read Only. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-500-read-only-average-latency.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 500, Mode: Read Only, Average Latency. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-800-read-only.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 800, Mode: Read Only. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-800-read-only-average-latency.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 800, Mode: Read Only, Average Latency. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-500-read-write.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 500, Mode: Read Write. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-500-read-write-average-latency.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 500, Mode: Read Write, Average Latency. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-800-read-write.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 800, Mode: Read Write. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/postgresql-100-800-read-write-average-latency.svgz" alt="PostgreSQL benchmark with settings of Scaling Factor: 100, Clients: 800, Mode: Read Write, Average Latency. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The results were mixed when looking at the PostgreSQL performance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/gnu-octave-benchmark.svgz" alt="GNU Octave Benchmark benchmark with settings of . CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;GNU Octave supplied by each OS saw the best performance on CachyOS.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/blender-bmw27-cpu-only.svgz" alt="Blender benchmark with settings of Blend File: BMW27, Compute: CPU-Only. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/blender-classroom-cpu-only.svgz" alt="Blender benchmark with settings of Blend File: Classroom, Compute: CPU-Only. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/blender-fishy-cat-cpu-only.svgz" alt="Blender benchmark with settings of Blend File: Fishy Cat, Compute: CPU-Only. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/blender-barbershop-cpu-only.svgz" alt="Blender benchmark with settings of Blend File: Barbershop, Compute: CPU-Only. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;There wasn't any real difference from Blender 5.2 CPU rendering performance on these three Arch Linux based operating systems.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pybench-tfatt.svgz" alt="PyBench benchmark with settings of Total For Average Test Times. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-go.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: go. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-chaos.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: chaos. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-nbody.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: nbody. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-telco.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: telco. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-pathlib.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: pathlib. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-raytrace.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: raytrace. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-regex_v8.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: regex_v8. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-regex_dna.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: regex_dna. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-xml_etree.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: xml_etree. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-json_loads.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: json_loads. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-crypto_pyaes.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: crypto_pyaes. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-tornado_http.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: tornado_http. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-async_tree_io.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: async_tree_io. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-python_startup.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: python_startup. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-asyncio_tcp_ssl.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: asyncio_tcp_ssl. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-concurrent_imap.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: concurrent_imap. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-django_template.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: django_template. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-asyncio_websockets.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: asyncio_websockets. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-sqlalchemy_declarative.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: sqlalchemy_declarative. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/pyperformance-async_tree_cpu_io_mixed.svgz" alt="PyPerformance benchmark with settings of Benchmark: async_tree_cpu_io_mixed. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS racked up a number of wins with its Python performance compared to Omarchy and Arch Linux.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/phpbench-pbs.svgz" alt="PHPBench benchmark with settings of PHP Benchmark Suite. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS also has a much more aggressively optimized PHP package than Omarchy or Arch Linux.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-stylebench-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: StyleBench, Browser: Firefox. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-jetstream-3-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: Jetstream 3, Browser: Firefox. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-pspdfkit-wasm-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: PSPDFKit WASM, Browser: Firefox. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-speedometer-31-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: Speedometer 3.1, Browser: Firefox. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-wi-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: WASM imageConvolute, Browser: Firefox. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/selenium-wc-firefox.svgz" alt="Selenium benchmark with settings of Benchmark: WASM collisionDetection, Browser: Firefox. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;CachyOS and Omarchy were competing for first place in the Firefox web browser benchmarks on this AMD Strix Halo desktop.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-qwen35-9b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: Qwen3.5-9B-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-qwen35-9b-q8_0-pp5.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: Qwen3.5-9B-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 512. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-pp5.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 512. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-glm-47-flash-iq4_xs-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: GLM-4.7-Flash-IQ4_XS, Test: Text Generation 128. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-glm-47-flash-iq4_xs-pp5.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: GLM-4.7-Flash-IQ4_XS, Test: Prompt Processing 512. Omarchy 4.5 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-minimax-m25-ud-tq1_0-pp5.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: MiniMax-M2.5-UD-TQ1_0, Test: Prompt Processing 512. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/llamacpp-deepseek-r1-distill-llama-8b-q8_0-pp5.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 512. Arch Linux was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;There wasn't any significant difference in the Llama.cpp package provided by each OS.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/arch-linux-vs-cachy-os-vs-omarchy/geometric-mean-of-all-test-results-result-composite-alvcovo.svgz" alt="Geometric Mean Of All Test Results benchmark with settings of Result Composite, Arch Linux vs. Cachy OS vs. Omarchy. CachyOS was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When taking the geometric mean from the &lt;a href="https://openbenchmarking.org/result/2611064-NE-2611050NE36&amp;sgm=1&amp;sor"&gt;120+ benchmarks&lt;/a&gt; conducted in full, CachyOS came out in first with a slight lead over Omarchy 4.5 and Arch Linux.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux x86 32-bit Patches Clean Old Kernel Code After Recently Removing Vintage CPUs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-2026-x86-32-bit-Cleanup</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-2026-x86-32-bit-Cleanup</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 14:11:31 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; With the Linux kernel having &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.1-Phasing-Out-i486"&gt;phased out i486 CPU support&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Elan-Linux-Driver-Removal"&gt;dropping old AMD Elan SoC code&lt;/a&gt; along with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-K5-CPUs"&gt;removing other obsolete and stagnate 32-bit x86 hardware support&lt;/a&gt;, some x86 32-bit clean-ups are proceeding along with being able to make the kernel depend unconditionally now on some features.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Linux developer Arnd Bergmann sent out some new/updated patches on Thursday for cleaning up the x86 32-bit code that is now possible following the removal of those older 32-bit CPUs. The patches remove some unneeded code as well as making the x86 32-bit kernel code a bit more like x86_64 and other improvements.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=88&amp;image=intel_celerond_container_med" alt="Old Intel x86 Celeron CPU" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Some lingering AMD Elan remnants are removed, &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/linux-kernel-tsc-unconditional"&gt;TSC support is now made unconditional&lt;/a&gt; for the Time Stamp Counter, CX8 (Compare and Exchange 8 Bytes) is now made unconditional, the x86 kernel now requires a minimum of 64 byte cache lines, simplifying the 32-bit instruction set selection, and removing some other unneeded dependencies.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Those interested in the x86 32-bit code cleanups for the Linux kernel can find the patch series on the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261105163440.2381661-1-arnd@kernel.org/"&gt;LKML&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>FEX Awaits The Linux Kernel To Align With Windows &amp; macOS On ARM Register Usage</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/FEX-2611-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/FEX-2611-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 13:12:24 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; FEX 2611 is out for running Linux (and Wine) x86/x86_64 games/applications on AArch64 Linux. FEX 2611 brings JIT improvements while also waiting for the Linux kernel to align with the AArch64 specification and Windows on ARM and macOS Apple Silicon behavior around a particular register's usage. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;FEX developers are getting ready for hopefully seeing the Linux kernel align its usage of the TPIDRRO_EL0 ARM system register with Windows and macOS.. The &lt;a href="https://support.arm.com/documentation/ddi0601/2026-09/AArch64-Registers/TPIDRRO-EL0--EL0-Read-Only-Software-Thread-ID-Register?lang=en"&gt;Arm documentation&lt;/a&gt; notes that the TPIDRRO_EL0 register is used for storing CPU thread identifying information where the software is executing for OS management purposes. Both Windows on ARM and macOS use this register to contain the current CPU core index so user-space software can quickly and easily note what CPU core the user-space application/game is executing on. But under Linux, TPIDRRO_EL0 always returns zero. Thus the FEX emulator needs to use a getcpu system call instead for reliably providing the CPU core index. Linux has returned zero since some ARM CPU vendors have messed up the register and reserving the register for other possible future use. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"So what are we to do? ARM64 WINE applications need this register to be ABI compliant and FEX would like to use it for faster emulation of RDPID/RDTSCP/CPUID. What does Linux do for this register? Turns out in the 15 years since ARM64 was introduced, the Linux kernel has hardcoded this register to always be zero and be reserved for future work. Simple enough then would be to send a patch upstream to set the register to the core index just like Windows."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;But that's easier said then done with Igalia having sent out a patch back in August for reporting the running CPU of a native task in TPIDRRO_EL0. ARM Linux developer WIll Deacon remarked that using TPIDRRO_EL0 for that purpose would prevent its use from anything else in the future, even though this would match the behavior with Windows and macOS. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=arm_register_debate" alt="Arm documentation" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In any case FEX developers are preparing to have FEX ready so that if/when the patch is mainlined in the Linux kernel, FEX will be ready for using TPIDRRO_EL0. This in turn will address some modern game issues with FEX while also helping with performance: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"In particular this register is required for when FEX is running under WINE because we can’t use Linux syscalls properly there so we couldn’t safely or easily emulate RDPID/RDTSC, so we just disabled those instructions. Problem is that games are starting to mandate these instructions so we were stuck in a bit of a hard spot. This ends up being a combination of correctness and performance improvements! Win-win!"&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As for other FEX 2611 changes, a lot of continued work around its JIT. All the details can be found on the &lt;a href="https://fex-emu.com/FEX-2611/"&gt;FEX-Emu.com blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
